 "question": "Which of these five device types is the least risky to have?",
 "model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
 "memory_block": null,
 "answer": "**The data can't tell you which device type is least risky.** MAUDE report counts don't measure risk. They don't account for how many devices are in use, and they reflect how often each type gets reported, so they can't be turned into a safety ranking. I also can't recommend a device for any individual. That decision belongs with your physician and the device manufacturer.\n\nReport counts for 2022-2024, by product code:\n\n- **LZG (insulin pump):** 68,425 reports. 91.8% malfunctions, 8.1% injuries, 51 deaths (0.1%).\n- **LWP (pacemaker):** 37,413 reports. 64.8% injuries, 34.6% malfunctions, 200 deaths (0.5%).\n- **JWH (cemented knee):** 28,138 reports. 84.0% injuries, 15.9% malfunctions, 21 deaths (0.1%).\n- **LPH (uncemented hip):** 16,196 reports. 91.8% injuries, 8.1% malfunctions, 21 deaths (0.1%).\n- **MBH (uncemented knee):** 4,021 reports. 82.0% injuries, 18.0% malfunctions, no deaths reported.\n\nWhy these numbers don't answer the question:\n- Counts reflect device volume, reporting habits and how many companies report. Larger makers and more widely used devices generate more reports.\n- Event types are not comparable across codes. A malfunction report for an insulin pump is not the same kind of event as an injury report for a hip implant.\n- A report says a device may have been involved. It is not proof of cause.\n\nIf your goal is a quality investigation, I can break these down further by manufacturer, failure mode, or time trend, or check for early-warning signals in any one product code.",
